Proof. We’ll use induction on the degree of f . Clearly if f is linear the result is true. Assume therefore that deg(f ) > 1. By the above theorem we may find a finite field extension K/F such that ∃ α ∈ K such that f (α) = 0. This implies that f = (X − α)g for some g ∈ K[X]. By construction deg(g) < deg(f ). By induction we know that there is a finite field extension E/K in which g, and thus f , splits into linear factors. Because both E/K and K/F are finite E/F is finite.
